[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: [slug:dag_lite.dag.critical_path.longest_path] [viol:pret_qa.overspecified_oracle] Facet: critical path tie resolution Witness: nodes='alpha,beta,gamma,delta', durations=None, returned=['alpha','beta','delta'], asserted=['alpha','gamma','delta'], weight=3.0] The real test test_graph_analysis_scenarios pins the default-duration critical path to ['alpha','gamma','delta'] for a graph where alpha precedes both beta and gamma and both precede delta, so the graph admits two distinct longest paths of equal weight 3.0; low/dag_engine_lite.pyi requires only that compute_critical_path return a longest path and its cumulative weight and contracts no tie-break between equal-weight longest paths (a tie-break is contracted only for topological_sort), so the assertion rejects an equally valid maximal path and overspecifies the oracle. The same module also asserts report and scheduler values against a separately resolved DAG service through sut(DAG) instead of the contracted report contents, an uncontracted assumption about service identity that makes test_runner_success_scenarios, test_runner_fail_fast_scenarios, test_runner_skip_downstream_scenarios, and test_scheduler_wave_scenarios order-dependent and self-inconsistent (reported total_nodes, results membership, and state-map membership disagree only because the oracle graph is not the graph whose nodes were registered).
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Resolved pret_qa.overspecified_oracle feedback. (1) Critical path tie resolution: the default-duration scenario no longer pins a tie-broken node sequence; it now asserts the cumulative weight plus only the contracted longest-path properties (starts at the sole root alpha, ends at the sole leaf delta, and every consecutive pair is a real dependency edge), since low/dag_engine_lite.pyi contracts no tie-break for equal-weight longest paths; the explicit-duration case keeps its exact path because 8.0 is the unique maximum there. (2) Removed the uncontracted cross-service sut(DAG) oracle: DAGRunner.run now checks report-intrinsic contracts (non-negative wall clock, len(results) <= total_nodes, NodeResult states/ids consistent, len(critical_path) <= total_nodes) and WaveScheduler.get_all_states checks its own state map (string keys, NodeState values), so runner and scheduler scenarios no longer depend on separately resolved service identity. Runner scenarios also assert contracted tuple fields (failed/skipped/succeeded/cancelled) and all-results state sweeps instead of indexing specific result keys.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: [slug:dag_lite.runner.run.consolidated_report] [viol:pret_qa.uncontracted_state_rule] Facet: results mapping element type Witness: nodes='alpha,beta,gamma', policy=CONTINUE_ON_ERROR, element_type=NodeResult, compared_to=NodeState.SUCCEEDED, failing_assert_lines=407/420-423/434-437] The all-results state sweeps introduced in test_runner_success_scenarios, test_runner_skip_downstream_scenarios, and test_runner_fail_fast_scenarios iterate report.results.values() and compare each element directly to a NodeState member, but low/dag_engine_lite.pyi declares DAGRunReport.results as a mapping of node identifier to NodeResult records whose state field carries the NodeState, so each iterated element is a NodeResult record and the identity comparison can never hold regardless of implementation behavior; the contracted assertions that precede each sweep (total_nodes, succeeded/failed/skipped/cancelled tuples, critical_path, wall clock) all pass, so the newly added sweeps are the sole remaining defect: they assert a state rule on the consolidated report that the specification does not state, which is why the earlier overspecified-oracle feedback was addressed but verification still fails.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Resolved pret_qa.uncontracted_state_rule feedback on dag_lite.runner.run.consolidated_report: removed the three all-results sweeps that iterated report.results.values() and compared each NodeResult record directly against a NodeState member (an impossible identity comparison, and a state rule the consolidated-report contract does not state). The runner scenarios now assert only contracted report fields: total_nodes, succeeded/failed/skipped/cancelled node tuples, critical_path, and non-negative wall clock. Sibling audit: the only remaining results-mapping assertion is in the DAGRunner.run transition and reads result.state (the NodeResult.state field declared by low/dag_engine_lite.pyi), so it checks the record type contract rather than an undeclared state rule; get_all_states still checks its own state map keys/values.
